<h2>The trap case</h2>
<p>The CBI registered the case in April 2021 on a complaint by an allottee of an industrial plot who was pursuing execution of a lease deed with DSIIDC. The prosecution alleged that middlemen demanded a bribe to move the file through the appellant, the dealing clerk, and that in the trap one middleman was caught accepting Rs 10,000. The link to the appellant rested on a phone call said to confirm the file&rsquo;s approval and an alleged instruction not to disclose it. The appellant was convicted and sentenced in July 2026, paid the fine, and appealed.</p>

<h2>Why the sentence was suspended</h2>
<p>Three strands came together, each drawn from the record rather than argument:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>No demand, on the prosecution&rsquo;s own witnesses.</strong> The complainant and the shadow witness deposed that the appellant made no demand. The judgment invokes the Supreme Court&rsquo;s ruling in <em>B. Jayaraj</em>: demand is a sine qua non of the offence. Para 16 records the result plainly — &ldquo;Thus, a prima facie case has been made out by the appellant.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No recovery, no mention.</strong> The investigating officer accepted in evidence that the verification report did not mention the appellant and that nothing was recovered from him.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Appeal timelines.</strong> In the court&rsquo;s words (para 17), &ldquo;Where the chance of the appeal being heard in the near future is remote, that is a valid consideration&rdquo; — a four-year term could be substantially served before the appeal is ever reached.</div>
</div>
<p>The court also noted the appellant&rsquo;s conduct: he had remained on bail throughout the trial without misuse. The application was allowed on a bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety and the usual reporting, address and travel conditions, with the observations expressly confined to the suspension question.</p>

<h2>Suspension in fixed-term sentences: the working rule</h2>
<p>The decision applies a principle that recurs across appellate practice in Delhi: in fixed-term sentence cases — as opposed to life sentences — suspension is approached with relative liberality, because the right of appeal loses meaning if the sentence is served while the appeal waits. The appellant must still show something on the record; here, the gap between the trap&rsquo;s paperwork and the appellant&rsquo;s role supplied it. Arguments on electronic-evidence certification and prior sanction, also raised, were left for the appeal itself.</p>

<h2>Practical notes for convicts and complainants</h2>
<p>For a convicted appellant, the suspension application is often the most urgent filing after judgment: it should marshal the specific depositions and documents that create the prima facie doubt, state custody dates precisely, and demonstrate clean conduct on bail during trial. For the prosecution, the case is a reminder that trap convictions which rest on inference rather than proved demand are vulnerable at the suspension stage, well before the appeal is decided. Nothing in the order forecasts the outcome of the appeal — that question remains entirely open.</p>
